<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">Physics · NLP · Time Series · ML</div><h1>Predictive Maintenance for the ATLAS Tile Calorimeter</h1><p class="section-lead">Technical work based on the MSc dissertation “Alert Signal Classification and Prediction Using Natural Language Processing for the Tile Calorimeter of ATLAS.”</p></div></section>
<section><div class="container content">
<h2>Problem</h2>
<p>The study asks whether archived ATLAS Tile Calorimeter Detector Control System alarm logs can provide a predictive-maintenance layer in addition to their existing monitoring and protection role.</p>

<h2>Workflow</h2>
<ul>
<li>Select Error-level DCS alarms and normalise the semi-structured alarm text.</li>
<li>Use targeted NLP and rule-based extraction to recover detector partition, hardware and module information.</li>
<li>Consolidate alarms into five classes: <strong>ALERT, EMR, NO CONNECTION, NO TOGGLE and TRIPPED</strong>.</li>
<li>Construct module-level alarm-count time series for forecasting and partition-level sequences for dominant alarm-type classification.</li>
<li>Preserve chronological order, scale from the training portion, generate sliding-window sequences, tune LSTM models and evaluate forecasting and multiclass classification.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Results</h2>
<ul>
<li>LSTM alarm-count forecasting produced MAPE of approximately <strong>12.8–22.9%</strong> across the four selected forecasting cases.</li>
<li>The raw dominant-alarm classifier achieved <strong>88% accuracy</strong>, but the result was strongly affected by class imbalance.</li>
<li>After chattering-alarm reduction, accuracy was <strong>75%</strong> while macro-F1 improved from <strong>0.27 to 0.57</strong>, giving a more informative view of minority alarm classes.</li>
<li>TileCal alarm activity was strongly spatially non-uniform across partitions and modules.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">The LBA forecasting result is interpreted cautiously because overlapping sequences near the chronological split reduced independence between training and evaluation samples.</div>

<h2>Scope & interpretation</h2>
<p>This is a <strong>proof-of-concept predictive-maintenance study</strong>, not a deployed ATLAS operational system. Limited sample sizes, class imbalance, validation design and the absence of live DCS testing constrain operational conclusions. The underlying ATLAS/TileCal DCS alarm data are not released publicly; the repository provides the technical report, methodology, derived statistics and study results.</p>

<h2>What this case proves</h2>
<p>I can translate technically complex detector-control data into a machine-learning formulation, combine NLP extraction with time-series modelling, and interpret model results in the context of scientific operations rather than treating metrics in isolation.</p>

